  "content": "# Implementing a Bounded LRU Cache in Python\n\n## Overview\n\nA **Least Recently Used (LRU) cache** is a memory management policy that discards the least recently used items when the cache reaches its capacity. This tutorial demonstrates how to implement an efficient O(1) LRU cache using Python's `collections.OrderedDict`.\n\nThe key insight is that `OrderedDict` maintains insertion order and provides `move_to_end()` for reordering, which together allow us to simulate \"most recently used\" semantics in constant time.\n\n---\n\n## Complete Implementation\n\n```python\nfrom collections import OrderedDict\n\n\nclass LRUCache:\n    \"\"\"\n    A bounded LRU cache with O(1) get and put operations.\n    \n    When the cache reaches its capacity, the least recently used item\n    is evicted to make room for new entries.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, capacity: int):\n        \"\"\"\n        Initialize the LRU cache.\n        \n        Args:\n            capacity: Maximum number of items the cache can hold.\n                       Must be positive.\n            \n        Raises:\n            ValueError: If capacity is less than 1.\n        \"\"\"\n        if capacity < 1:\n            raise ValueError(\"Capacity must be positive\")\n        \n        self.capacity = capacity\n        self.cache: OrderedDict = OrderedDict()\n\n    def get(self, key: object) -> object | None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Retrieve the value associated with the given key.\n        \n        If the key exists, it is marked as most recently used by moving\n        it to the end of the OrderedDict. If the key does not exist,\n        None is returned.\n        \n        Args:\n            key: The key to look up.\n            \n        Returns:\n            The value associated with the key, or None if not found.\n        \"\"\"\n        if key not in self.cache:\n            return None\n        \n        # Move the accessed key to the end (most recently used)\n        self.cache.move_to_end(key)\n        return self.cache[key]\n\n    def put(self, key: object, value: object) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Insert or update a key-value pair in the cache.\n        \n        If the key already exists, its value is updated and the entry\n        is moved to the end (most recently used). If the key is new\n        and the cache is at capacity, the least recently used item",
